    "path": "02-computer-science/programs/michigan-cs.md",
    "title": "Michigan — MS",
    "body": "# Michigan — MS\n\nChecked: 19 October 2026. Preliminary program research.\n\n## Ranking and degree route\n\n#11 in U.S. News Best Computer Science Schools · graduate departments, April 2026 release.\n\n33 departments occupy ranks 1–27. All seven schools tied at 27 are included. This is a graduate department ranking, not a ranking of the specific master’s routes listed here.\n\nRanking provenance: [IvyPath Advisory](https://www.ivypath-advisory.com/ranking/major-cs-us/). The publisher’s direct table was inaccessible; the position is secondary-source reported.\n\nUniversity: University of Michigan, Ann Arbor\n\nDegree route: MS / MSE in Computer Science and Engineering\n\nApplication status: Route identified · 2027 eligibility to verify\n\n## Academic direction\n\nCSE master’s requires 30 credits; thesis optional; normally finished in 1.5–2 years. MS vs MSE is primarily naming/eligibility by prior engineering bachelor’s.\n\nOrientation: 30 credits · Optional thesis · 1.5–2 years\n\nFaculty and supervision: Individual faculty specialties and master’s supervision access have not yet been verified.\n\n## Tuition and application timing\n\n$68,038 — Academic-year tuition (College of Engineering graduate, nonresident full-time × 2 full terms); 2026–27.\n\n2026–27 reference, not a Fall 2027 quote or full-degree total.\n\nRegistrar College of Engineering Graduate Full Term 2026–27: Michigan Non-Resident 9+ (Full-Time) $34,019 per full term; Michigan Resident $18,308. Typical academic year = Fall + Winter full terms → nonresident $68,038. CSE MS/MSE uses Engineering graduate rates. Master’s funding is not guaranteed at admission.\n\nApplication timing: Apps reopen mid-Sep (Rackham; late Aug/early Sep noted) · Master's deadline 15 Jan · 11:59 p.m. ET (FA 2027 cycle referenced on page). Confirm deadline time zone and personal requirements on the official page.\n\n## Application requirements\n\nAs of 2026-10-27.\n\n- GRE: not_accepted — CSE How to Apply / FAQs: GRE scores are neither required nor considered / not expected; committee will not consider any submitted scores.\n- GMAT: not_required — Not used for CSE Rackham MS/MSE admissions.\n- TOEFL: required — min 84 — Non-native English speakers must demonstrate proficiency unless Rackham exemption met. Michigan Engineering Academic Services minima (College page CSE applicants are directed to follow): TOEFL iBT overall 84 before 2026-01-21 and 4.5 on/after 2026-01-21; ETS code 1839. CSE apply page points to Rackham exemptions.\n- IELTS: required — min 6.5 — Academic IELTS minimum overall 6.5 (Michigan Engineering Academic Services English proficiency table). Official electronic scores to University of Michigan–Ann Arbor.\n- English waiver: Rackham exemptions include: native English speaker; Puerto Rico applicant; completed all undergraduate education and earned undergraduate degree at English-only instruction institution; completed all graduate education and earned graduate degree at English-only instruction institution; current U-M student. One-year research-only master's typically does not qualify.\n- GPA: recommended — minimum 3.5 — scale 4.0 — CSE Graduate Admissions: successful applicants usually have undergraduate GPA of at least 3.5/4.0 (not a hard requirement).\n- Letters of recommendation: required — count 3 — Three letters via Rackham online recommendation system only (no email/mail/Interfolio). Suggested ≥2 from academia.\n- Statement of purpose: required — also known as statement of purpose; personal statement — Academic Statement of Purpose (≤3 pages) on academic/research background, career goals, and fit with CSE. Separate Personal Statement (≤500 words) also required—not identical to SOP.\n- Resume: required — Resume highlighting education, work, research publications, teaching, professional activities, volunteer service, honors/awards.\n- Transcripts: required — Upload electronic official transcripts for each bachelor’s/master’s/professional/doctoral degree earned or in progress; official to Rackham if recommended/admitted. Non-U.S.: follow Rackham country credentials guidance.\n- Interview: unknown — Interview not listed on CSE How to Apply checklist or admissions FAQs reviewed.\n- Fee: $90 — waivers: Rackham fee waivers for U.S. citizens/permanent residents with demonstrated financial need (Pell and/or SAI <$1,500); request before submission. Individual programs may also offer waivers. — Rackham: $75 U.S. citizens/permanent residents; $90 non-U.S. citizens; $10 current Rackham students. amountUsd records international rate commonly used for intl CS applicants.\n- Other: Master's (MS/MSE) deadline January 15 (Fall only); PhD Dec 15; SUGS separate.\n- Other: MS vs MSE: same requirements; MSE only if bachelor's in engineering.\n- Other: Admission to Master's typically without departmental financial aid.\n- Other: Cannot hold same CS MS/PhD already earned elsewhere and re-apply for same degree at Michigan.\n\nGRE is not_accepted (not considered), not merely optional. Include both SOP and Personal Statement.\n\n- [CSE How to Apply](https://cse.engin.umich.edu/academics/graduate/admissions/apply/)\n- [CSE Graduate Admissions](https://cse.engin.umich.edu/academics/graduate/admissions/)\n- [CSE Graduate Admissions FAQs](https://cse.engin.umich.edu/academics/graduate/admissions/faqs/)\n- [Master's in CSE overview](https://cse.engin.umich.edu/academics/graduate/graduate-programs/masters-in-cse/)\n- [Rackham Application Fee](https://rackham.umich.edu/admissions/applying/application-fee-and-payment/)\n- [Rackham Tests / English](https://rackham.umich.edu/admissions/applying/tests/)\n- [Michigan Engineering Prepare Application Materials (English minima)](https://academicservices.engin.umich.edu/prepare-your-application-materials/)\n\n## Internships\n\nProgram-specific internship structure, placement rates and international-student outcomes have not been verified. Ranking or location alone does not establish how easy an internship is to obtain.\n\n## Funding\n\nCSE master’s pages historically note limited funding; do not assume PhD support. Specific 2026–27 MS funding guarantees not verified on tuition page.\n\nShared scholarship IDs: SCH-015, SCH-016, SCH-017, SCH-018, SCH-019, SCH-020, SCH-021, SCH-038, SCH-039, SCH-040, SCH-041, SCH-042, SCH-043, SCH-044, SCH-045, SCH-059, SCH-062, SCH-064, SCH-072, SCH-073, SCH-074, SCH-079, SCH-080, SCH-081, SCH-084. See the [scholarship library](../../04-scholarships/README.md).\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Master’s in CSE](https://cse.engin.umich.edu/academics/graduate/graduate-programs/masters-in-cse/)\n- [Official tuition / budget](https://ro.umich.edu/tuition-residency/tuition-fees/2026-2027/graduate/full-term/college-engineering-graduate-full-term)\n- [CSE graduate admissions](https://cse.engin.umich.edu/academics/graduate/admissions/)\n- [CSE how to apply](https://cse.engin.umich.edu/academics/graduate/admissions/apply/)\n- [U.S. News positions via IvyPath Advisory](https://www.ivypath-advisory.com/ranking/major-cs-us/)\n\n## Outstanding evidence\n\n- Fall 2027 tuition, mandatory fees and total degree budget\n- Individual faculty fit and availability for master’s supervision\n- Internship outcomes with cohort, denominator and international-student breakdown\n- Personal scholarship eligibility and deadlines\n- THE / QS comparison in the correct category\n"
