    "path": "05-finance/programs/chicago-booth-mif.md",
    "title": "Chicago Booth — MSF",
    "body": "# Chicago Booth — MSF\n\nChecked: 19 October 2026. Preliminary program research.\n\n## Ranking and degree route\n\nUnranked in TFE Times Best Master's of Finance (as of 2026-10-26).\n\nBatch 1–4 (2026-10-26): TFE Times 2026 ranks 1–30 ingested. Locked unranked adds on this slate: florida-msft, chicago-booth-mif, columbia-msfe. US News Finance MBA specialty is not used as an MSF program rank. Quant/MFE is a separate quant-mfe track ranked by QuantNet 2026; those eight rows keep tfeRank2026 null and are not merged into TFE ranks 1–30.\n\nRanking provenance: [TFE Times](https://tfetimes.com/best-finance-program-rankings/).\n\nUniversity: University of Chicago\n\nDegree route: Asness and Liew Master in Finance (MiF)\n\nApplication status: Route identified · 2027 eligibility to verify\n\n## Academic direction\n\nChicago Booth MiF (locked add): STEM-eligible, 15 mo / 4 quarters. Early Review from Oct 1’26; Round One Jan 7’27. Total tuition Aut 2026 matriculants $123,470 (proxy).\n\nOrientation: Autumn · 15 months · STEM-eligible · locked add (no TFE rank)\n\nFaculty and supervision: Individual faculty specialties and master’s supervision access have not yet been verified.\n\n## Tuition and application timing\n\n$123,470 — Total tuition four-quarter program — Autumn 2026 matriculants (proxy for Autumn 2027 until updated); 2026–27 proxy (Autumn 2026 matriculant total; Autumn 2027 rate not published) · 2026/27 (Autumn 2026 matriculant totals on Booth MiF Cost page).\n\nAsness and Liew Master in Finance (MiF); STEM-eligible for OPT (FAQ); 15 months / four quarters; summer internship after first three quarters. Accelerated 10-month track discontinued. Autumn 2027 cohort rates may change — confirm when updated. Locked add (no TFE 2026 rank). AUTUMN MATRICULATION with a summer internship between academic terms (not a summer start). Tuition is the Autumn 2026 matriculant proxy until the Autumn 2027 rate is published.\n\nThree-quarter COA table tuition (not the four-quarter program total): $93,282. Catalog tuition is the Autumn 2026 four-quarter total proxy. Mandatory fees reference: $2,674. From 2026/27 Estimated COA (Three Quarters): Student Services Fee $1,560 + Lifetime Credentials Fee $84 + Administrative Service Fee $1,030 = $2,674. Full tuition charged over four quarterly installments. Books $549 in 3-quarter COA. Health insurance allowance embedded in Misc. Personal Expenses; university health rates TBD on page. Asness and Liew Master in Finance (MiF); STEM-eligible for OPT (FAQ); 15 months / four quarters; summer internship after first three quarters. Accelerated 10-month track discontinued. Autumn 2027 cohort rates may change — confirm when updated. Locked add (no TFE 2026 rank). Living cost band from official COA: $35,580 (Booth MiF 2026/27 Estimated COA (Three Quarters): Housing and Food $26,010 + Misc. Personal Expenses $7,770 + Transportation $1,800 = $35,580. Fourth quarter / internship summer living not separately itemized on page.). AUTUMN MATRICULATION with a summer internship between academic terms (not a summer start). Tuition is the Autumn 2026 matriculant proxy until the Autumn 2027 rate is published.\n\nApplication timing: Early Review · 2026-10-01 · Starting Oct 1, 2026; decisions rolling until mid-December 2026; submit by 11:59 p.m. CT. Confirm deadline time zone and personal requirements on the official page.\n\n## Application requirements\n\nAs of 2026-10-27.\n\n- GRE: required — Valid GMAT or GRE required; no preference. Test waiver if current UChicago undergraduate or graduated from a UChicago program. Current students in UChicago graduate programs NOT eligible for waiver.\n- GMAT: required — GMAT or GRE required except UChicago undergrad/alumni waiver (not for current UChicago grad students).\n- TOEFL: required — International non-native English speakers: TOEFL, IELTS Academic, or PTE. TOEFL code 1832-02. Scores valid 2 years. At-home/online proctored accepted. No numeric minimum published on how-to-apply page.\n- IELTS: required — IELTS Academic accepted; no numeric minimum on how-to-apply page.\n- English waiver: Exempt if undergrad/postgrad degree from listed countries (incl. Antigua & Barbuda, Australia, Bahamas, Barbados, Canada, Dominica, Grenada, Guyana, Hong Kong, Ireland, Jamaica, New Zealand, St. Kitts & Nevis, St. Lucia, St. Vincent & Grenadines, Singapore, South Africa, Trinidad & Tobago, UK, US); OR degree outside those countries with English as only language of instruction; OR living and working full-time in U.S. ≥2 years. Citizenship alone does not exempt. Committee may still require a score.\n- GPA: not_stated — No minimum GPA stated. Holistic review of background, experiences, grades, and tests.\n- Letters of recommendation: required — count 2 — Two letters from people who know you well with specific examples of academic performance/contributions; prefer substance over title. Online form only (no mail/fax).\n- Statement of purpose: required — also known as Essay questions + video essay — Two essays: (1) How Booth MiF helps immediate and long-term career goals (max 300 words); (2) Something about who you are outside classroom/work—activity pursued for its own sake (max 400 words). Optional clarification section. Plus required video essay ≤60 seconds introducing yourself and a non-career pursuit.\n- Resume: not_stated — Resume/CV not listed on MiF How to Apply checklist.\n- Transcripts: required — Unofficial scans of all postsecondary transcripts (both sides) with application; official transcripts upon admission. Non-English records need official record + authorized English translation.\n- Interview: by_invitation — Invitation-only after application evaluation; 100% virtual with admissions staff; required for admission (interview ≠ guarantee). Also brief video simulation exercise with interview invite.\n- Fee: How-to-apply checklist does not state an application fee amount; confirm in online application system.\n- Other: Recommended advanced quantitative coursework and coding exposure (e.g., Linear Algebra, Statistics, Multivariable Calculus, Python, C++).\n- Other: Early Review from Oct 1, 2026 (rolling to mid-Dec); Round One Jan 7, 2027.\n- Other: Submit by 11:59 p.m. CT on deadline day.\n\nPrepare for video essay and possible interview simulation. UChicago undergrads/alumni should claim test waiver; others must submit GMAT/GRE.\n\n- [How to apply](https://www.chicagobooth.edu/master-in-finance/admissions/how-to-apply)\n- [MiF FAQ](https://www.chicagobooth.edu/master-in-finance/faq)\n\n## Internships\n\nProgram-specific internship structure, placement rates and international-student outcomes have not been verified. Ranking or location alone does not establish how easy an internship is to obtain.\n\n## Funding\n\nProgram-tied funding verified 2026-10-26: SCH-126. MBA-only Forté, ROMBA, Consortium, and Toigo awards (SCH-034–037) are not attached.\n\nShared scholarship IDs: SCH-126. See the [scholarship library](../../04-scholarships/README.md).\n\n## Sources\n\n- [How to apply / deadlines](https://www.chicagobooth.edu/master-in-finance/admissions/how-to-apply)\n- [Official tuition / budget](https://www.chicagobooth.edu/master-in-finance/admissions/cost-financial-aid)\n- [FAQ / STEM / duration](https://www.chicagobooth.edu/master-in-finance/faq)\n- [TFE Times 2026 MSF rank via TFE Times](https://tfetimes.com/best-finance-program-rankings/)\n\n## Outstanding evidence\n\n- Fall 2027 tuition, mandatory fees and total degree budget\n- Individual faculty fit and availability for master’s supervision\n- Internship outcomes with cohort, denominator and international-student breakdown\n- Personal scholarship eligibility and deadlines\n- THE / QS comparison in the correct category\n"
