    "path": "05-finance/programs/cmu-mscf.md",
    "title": "Carnegie Mellon University — MSF",
    "body": "# Carnegie Mellon University — MSF\n\nChecked: 19 October 2026. Preliminary program research.\n\n## Ranking and degree route\n\n#3 in QuantNet 2026 Ranking of Best Financial Engineering Programs (as of 2026-10-26).\n\nSeparate quant-mfe track. Not a TFE Times MSF rank. tfeRank2026 is null on every row. Stanford ICME MCF is not on the 2026 QuantNet list (QuantNet rank null).\n\nRanking provenance: [IBKR Quant News](https://www.interactivebrokers.com/campus/ibkr-quant-news/2026-quantnet-ranking-of-best-financial-engineering-programs/).\n\nUniversity: Carnegie Mellon University\n\nDegree route: Master of Science in Computational Finance (MSCF)\n\nApplication status: Fall 2027 (Aug orientation; Fall-only entry)\n\n## Academic direction\n\nCMU MSCF: Fall-only; R1 Dec 1'26 / R2 Mar 1'27. Tuition $71,800/AY 2026–27. ~16 mo Pittsburgh or NYC. STEM. QuantNet #3.\n\nOrientation: Fall start · ~16 mo · QuantNet #3 · SEPARATE TRACK\n\nFaculty and supervision: Individual faculty specialties and master’s supervision access have not yet been verified.\n\n## Tuition and application timing\n\n$71,800 — MSCF tuition academic year 2026–27 (Fall+Spring) Pittsburgh or NYC — $35,900/semester; 2026–27 AY.\n\nSEPARATE TRACK quant-mfe. Fall-only entry; mandatory Aug orientation from Aug 1; required summer internship. STEM (verify CIP on I-20). QuantNet ~$104.6k total not used as official. PIT COA total $110,408 / NYC $112,606 incl. living/insurance/loan fees (as of Jul/Jun 2026 on SFS). Fall 2027 intake context.\n\nMandatory fees reference: $1,040. Pittsburgh AY required fees: Student Activities $248 + Transportation $302 + Technology $490 = $1,040. NYC: Technology $490 (no activities/transport on SFS table). Health Insurance PIT $3,352 / NYC $3,258 (AY). Summer Session rates set by college — total program tuition over ~16 mo (3 academic semesters + required summer internship) not published as a flat sticker; illustrative 3×$35,900=$107,700 unverified for summer billing. SEPARATE TRACK quant-mfe. Fall-only entry; mandatory Aug orientation from Aug 1; required summer internship. STEM (verify CIP on I-20). QuantNet ~$104.6k total not used as official. PIT COA total $110,408 / NYC $112,606 incl. living/insurance/loan fees (as of Jul/Jun 2026 on SFS). CMU SFS MSCF: $71,800 AY tuition ($35,900/sem). Program ~16 months (Fall, Spring, required summer internship, final Fall). Total program tuition depends on summer billing — re-confirm with MSCF; QuantNet lists ~$104.6k (not official). Living cost band from official COA: $30,504–$33,252 (CMU SFS MSCF 2026–27 AY living: PIT Housing $14,752 + Food $8,664 + Personal $4,098 + Travel $2,990 = $30,504; NYC Housing $15,952 + Food $9,570 + Personal $4,142 + Travel $3,588 = $33,252 (excludes books $2,212 and health insurance).).\n\nApplication timing: Round 1 application · 2026-12-01. Confirm deadline time zone and personal requirements on the official page.\n\n## Application requirements\n\nAs of 2026-10-27.\n\n- GRE: required — Official valid GRE or GMAT required (not both). Scores within 5 years and valid at application; accept arrival within 2 weeks of deadline. Self-report highest; GRE program code 7383. Application not reviewed without official score.\n- GMAT: required — GMAT accepted in lieu of GRE (code MSCF/Tepper 69H-XH-17). Either GRE or GMAT required.\n- TOEFL: required — International candidates must take Duolingo, TOEFL (code B522 Carnegie Mellon–MSCF), or IELTS unless waiver criteria met. Scores valid 2 years at application. No numeric minimum posted on MSCF admissions page.\n- IELTS: required — Accepted; select Carnegie Mellon University and Computational Finance. Minimum not posted on MSCF admissions page.\n- English waiver: Waiver if ≥2 years at undergrad institution earning bachelor’s where sole language of instruction was English (by Jul 31 of entry-year context per page wording—verify cycle year) OR graduate degree taught entirely in English. Documentation rules apply for non-U.S./non-English-official-language countries.\n- GPA: not_stated — No minimum GPA on MSCF admissions page; class profile shows average GPA 3.80 (descriptive, not a cutoff).\n- Letters of recommendation: required — count 3 — Three LORs; prefer ability to speak to math/stats/CS/applied finance, English communication, teamwork. Recent students: ≥1 academic; prefer ≥1 professional. App not forwarded until all 3 received.\n- Statement of purpose: required — also known as essays / video essay — Two short-answer essays (350 words max each) after selecting post-MSCF career path; plus required 90-second video essay; optional essay ≤2 pages.\n- Resume: required — 1–2 pages; standard 12-pt font; must include month/year start and end for each position.\n- Transcripts: required — Upload legible scans of official transcripts/degree certificates for ALL institutions (incl. exchange/transfer). Internet printouts/unofficial copies not accepted for upload standard stated. Non-English: official translation; WES evaluations not accepted (WES standards as guideline only). Official sealed upon admit.\n- Interview: by_invitation — Invite-only on-campus or Zoom; requests for interview not considered. Clarifies quant prep, career fit, professional communication.\n- Fee: $125 — waivers: Fee waived for reapplicants who applied for 2026 when reapplying for Fall 2027. — $125 payable online by credit card at submission.\n- Other: Prerequisites: Calc I–II, linear algebra, calculus-based probability, ≥1 semester OOP (C++/Java/Python/C#); remediation before enrollment possible.\n- Other: Four-year bachelor’s or foreign equivalent required before start.\n- Other: Fall-only; mandatory on-campus Orientation from Aug 1.\n- Other: Fall 2027 rounds: R1 Dec 1 2026; R2 Mar 1 2027.\n\nFall-only with Aug 1 orientation and required summer internship path. GRE/GMAT hard required.\n\n- [MSCF Admissions](https://www.cmu.edu/mscf/admissions)\n- [MSCF Admissions FAQ](https://www.cmu.edu/mscf/admissions/faq)\n\n## Internships\n\nProgram-specific internship structure, placement rates and international-student outcomes have not been verified. Ranking or location alone does not establish how easy an internship is to obtain.\n\n## Funding\n\nProgram-tied funding verified 2026-10-26: SCH-141.\n\nShared scholarship IDs: SCH-141. See the [scholarship library](../../04-scholarships/README.md).\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Admissions / deadlines](https://www.cmu.edu/mscf/admissions)\n- [FAQ (duration, internship, Fall-only)](https://www.cmu.edu/mscf/admissions/faq)\n- [Official tuition / budget](https://www.cmu.edu/sfs/tuition/graduate/mscf.html)\n- [Curriculum](https://www.cmu.edu/mscf/academics/curriculum)\n- [QuantNet 2026 MFE ranking via IBKR Quant News](https://www.interactivebrokers.com/campus/ibkr-quant-news/2026-quantnet-ranking-of-best-financial-engineering-programs/)\n- [MSCF Tuition & Financial Aid](https://www.cmu.edu/mscf/admissions/tuition-financial-aid)\n\n## Outstanding evidence\n\n- Fall 2027 tuition, mandatory fees and total degree budget\n- Individual faculty fit and availability for master’s supervision\n- Internship outcomes with cohort, denominator and international-student breakdown\n- Personal scholarship eligibility and deadlines\n- THE / QS comparison in the correct category\n"
